 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two independent precision voltage comparators share one supply pair. Each has a PNP input stage and an output that is the open collector of an NPN transistor with its emitter at ground, so each output needs a pull-up resistor. The output levels suit TTL, DTL, ECL, MOS and CMOS logic."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "It runs from one supply of 2.0 Vdc to 36 Vdc or from split rails of ±1.0 Vdc to ±18 Vdc. Supply current is about 0.4 mA for both comparators and changes little with supply voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The common mode range reaches down to ground on a single supply and up to VCC - 1.5 V. The differential input range equals the supply voltage, and a differential input larger than the supply does not damage the inputs; no input should go below -0.3 V. Input ESD clamps add ruggedness without changing performance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Stability",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "With its high gain and wide bandwidth, the comparator can oscillate during output transitions from VOL to VOH when stray capacitance couples an output to an input. The remedy given is input resistance under 10 kΩ together with positive feedback of less than 10 mV. Unused pins are best tied to ground."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output current",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The output may sink up to 20 mA whatever the supply voltage. It survives a continuous short to ground, but a short to VCC can overheat it and destroy it."
  },
